Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 09:57:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 076/154] fix(sidebar): one owner for the resize seam, and
 guard the message surface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The seam had two handles with a gap between them: the native grip owned the
six pixels outside the sidebar, the page handle owned a ten-pixel strip inset
six pixels in, and nobody owned the sidebar's own last six. The cursor fell
back to the default there, which is what a user sees as a flicker. The grip now
reaches over the sidebar and the page handle is gone, along with its messages.
Two defects it was masking: the grip never followed the slot on a re-dock, and
the drag had no direction mirror, so dragging left on a right-docked sidebar
ran the width to its minimum instead of widening it.

Deleting the page handler orphaned SetSidebarWidthLive, which stops the resize
animation timer. The grip's live path did not, and the timer can fire mid-drag:
the edge-reveal zone ends exactly where the widened grab band begins, so
reaching the outer half of the band is itself the zone exit. The grip routes
through it now rather than the function being deleted.

Message-surface guards: the cross-profile mirror accepted any URL scheme while
its three siblings validated, so a renderer could load file:// or chrome:// into
another profile's pane; one settings handler read an argument with no type
guard, which aborts a release build; four settings pushes interpolated raw JSON
into JS source instead of parsing it; and the capture verbs ran on a message
alone, with no user-activation gate. The custom endpoint now refuses a local
address at connect time, so a rebinding host cannot receive the bearer key.

Comments naming atrium_browser_controller.mm and atrium_browser_window.mm are
retargeted - the Views port renamed both, and one file's own header line named
its .mm twin.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
